<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimbri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cimbri were an ancient people, probably from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, whose migration with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a> and others shook the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> from 113 BCE. They defeated Roman armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noreia">Noreia</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in 105 BCE, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s worst defeats, and wandered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Quintus Lutatius Catulus annihilated them at Vercellae in 101 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimbri.jpg" alt="Cimbri" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arch.-Stud. A.P. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germanic_Tribes_in_the_Roman_Imperial_Period.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between Arausio, now Orange, and the Rhône, the <b>Cimbri</b> under Boiorix and the Teutons under Teutobod defeated two Roman armies under the proconsul Quintus Servilius Caepio and the consul Gnaeus Mallius Maximus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar says the Aduatuci descended from six thousand <b>Cimbri</b> and Teutones left to guard the baggage of the great migration before 102 BCE, who settled on the middle Meuse.</blockquote><a href="/state/aduatuci">Aduatuci</a></li><li><blockquote>In 105 BCE the <b>Cimbri</b> and Teutones destroyed two Roman armies at Arausio in their land, one of Rome's worst defeats.</blockquote><a href="/state/cavares">Cavares</a></li><li><blockquote>They were divided into four cantons, and one of them, the Tigurini under Divico, joined the <b>Cimbri</b> and defeated a Roman army near Agen in 107 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>In 113 BCE Rome defended it against the migrating <b>Cimbri</b>, who defeated a Roman army at Noreia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Kingdom of Noricum</a></li><li><blockquote>In 107 BCE they rebelled with the help of the migrating <b>Cimbri</b> and Tigurini.</blockquote><a href="/state/volcae-tectosages">Volcae Tectosages</a></li></ul></section>
